Perhaps the resurrected character could automatically run for the
upstairs, grabbing any loot along the way (watch those stashes, boys
and girls). They should use also other means of escape (ie C?oT) if
available. If (s)he escapes the level alive, (s)he disappears entirely
from the game, along with any gear they were carrying, and the savegame
is created there - standing on the downstairs of the level above, with
the bones level below, and the other player (ie the guy who cast S2F)
having disappeared as well, along with any pets.

The question is, should the resurrected savegame get all the levels
from the original player's game, or should new levels be generated
randomly as needed?

If the first option, ascension could well become impossible if the
quest levels have already been generated, or the ascension-artifacts
(candlabrum etc) already looted. I think this is fine - the primary
goal of a resurrected character should not be to ascend, but simply to
escape the dungeon alive.

If the second option (new, random levels), then I think they should be
generated severely depleted of items, to reflect the fact that at least
two adventurers have passed this way already.

When the new game ends, the game should (somehow*) fish out the
original logfile/ record entry and amend them accordingly. for example:
"Dogscoff, escaped the dungeon after being stoned by a cockatrice on
Dlvl 13, and resurrected by Nippletwixt." Of course this assumes the
bones file wasn't imported from another system. In that case, the data
should be sent back to hearse so that the original user can have their
logfile updated accordingly.

*With the recent stats function added to hearse, I think maybe the game
needs to be altered so that bones files and logfile entries can be
positively tied together. This would allow YANIs like the one above to
actually work, and hearse to raid logfiles for additional information
when picking up bones files.



